format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

lesson atoms-are-everywhere
title: Atoms are everywhere
minutes: 4
xp: 40
teaches: u1-matter-is-atoms
teaches: u1-atom-scale

description:
    Everything you can touch, and the air around you, is made of atoms. You will see what counts as matter and what does not. Then you will feel how small an atom is.

cite ref-openstax-chem-ch1
supports: Matter has mass and takes up space; air is matter.

cite ref-openstax-chem-ch2
supports: Matter is made of atoms; atoms are too small to see.

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: What is everything made of?
figure: matter-vs-energy
figure_at: 1

body:
    Your hand, your phone and an apple are all [[matter]]. All matter is made of [[atom|atoms]]. Light and heat are different. They are energy, not matter.

card c03
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l1-sort-matter

card c12
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Air is matter too
figure: air-balloons
figure_at: 2

body:
    Air feels like nothing, but it is matter too. It is made of atoms. That is why a pumped-up ball weighs more than a flat one.

card c13
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l1-tom-air-empty
connected: true

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Far too small to see
figure: zoom-chain
figure_at: 2

body:
    Atoms are far too small to see, even with an ordinary microscope. You can zoom in on a skin cell. An atom is much, much smaller.

